Amylase is a digestive enzyme involved in the hydrolysis of complex carbohydrates (like starch) into simpler sugars. 
\begin{center} starch\ce{->[amylase]}glucose+maltose \end{center}

It is primarily produced by the pancreas and salivary glands aiding digestion. It is present in human saliva, and it was named after the Ancient Greek name for saliva. It exists in three different forms: $\alpha-$amylase, $\beta-$amylase, and $\gamma-$amylase.
$\alpha-$amylase is the major form of amylase found in humans and other mammals, also being the fastest. $\beta-$amylase is present in plants and microbes, being responsible for the ripening of fruit as it breaks starch into maltose, resulting in the sweet flavor of ripe fruit. $\gamma-$amylase, present in animals and microbes, is most active around pH 3, being the most acidic optimum pH of all amylases. 
